<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chichen Itza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 600–<a href="/years/1100">1100</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Chichén Itzá, Chichʼen Itzaʼ, Uuc Yabnal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chichen Itza was a large pre-Columbian city built by the Maya people, whose ruins lie in Yucatán, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valladolid">Valladolid</a> and Mérida. It was a major focal point of the Northern Maya Lowlands from the Late Classic into the early Postclassic, and in the 10th century rose to be a regional capital controlling the area from central Yucatán to the north coast. Its buildings mix central Mexican styles with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puuc">Puuc</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chenes">Chenes</a> styles of the region, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-castillo-chichen-itza">Temple of Kukulcán</a> (El Castillo), the Great Ball Court and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacred-cenote">Sacred Cenote</a>. It declined as a regional center by 1100, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-montejo-the-younger">Francisco de Montejo the Younger</a>, who tried to make it a Spanish capital, abandoned it in 1534.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chichen-itza.jpg" alt="Chichen Itza" width="330" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daniel Schwen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chichen_Itza_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Maya name means At the mouth of the well of the Itzá. The northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yucatan-peninsula">Yucatán Peninsula</a> is karst, and its rivers run underground, so water comes from sinkholes called cenotes. Four visible cenotes could have supplied water all year at Chichen Itza, of which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacred-cenote">Sacred Cenote</a> is the best known. According to sources written after the Spanish conquest, in Maya and Spanish, the Maya threw objects and human beings into it as worship of the rain god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaac">Chaac</a>. Edward Herbert Thompson dredged it from 1904 to 1910 and recovered gold, jade, pottery and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a>, as well as human remains with wounds consistent with sacrifice.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city's core took shape between 750 and 900, and its final layout dates from after 900. The earliest hieroglyphic date found there corresponds to 832 and the last, in the Osario temple, to 998. The city was founded by local Maya of northern Yucatán. The Itza, a Maya group of debated origins, began arriving around 800. Chichen Itza rose as the major centres of the southern lowlands declined, and in the 10th century it appears to have contributed to the collapse of Yaxuna and Coba. Between 900 and 1050 it was a powerful regional capital, trading by sea through its port of Isla Cerritos, obtaining obsidian from central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and gold from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, and taking wealth as tribute from cities it conquered.</p><p>Archaeologists in the late 1980s proposed that it was ruled by a council of elite lineages, a system called multepal, but that idea has since been questioned. Colonial Maya sources such as the Book of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/books-of-chilam-balam">Chilam Balam</a> of Chumayel say that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunac-ceel">Hunac Ceel</a>, ruler of Mayapan, conquered it in the 13th century. Archaeological data indicate instead that it had declined by 1100, before Mayapan rose.</p><p>In 1526 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-montejo">Francisco de Montejo</a> obtained a charter to conquer Yucatán. His son Francisco Montejo the Younger reached Chichen Itza, renamed it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciudad-real">Ciudad Real</a> and divided the land among his soldiers. The Maya besieged the Spanish among the ruins, and an assault lost Montejo 150 of his remaining troops. He abandoned the city in 1534, and by 1535 the Spanish had been driven from the peninsula.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The site core covers at least 5 square kilometres and has over 80 causeways, called sacbeob. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-castillo-chichen-itza">Temple of Kukulcán</a>, about 30 m high with nine terraces, was built over an older temple found in the 1930s. The buildings were once painted in red, green, blue and purple. John Lloyd Stephens's book of 1843 made the city known, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>'s national anthropology institute now cares for the ruins.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chichen Itza” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The League of Mayapan took shape in northern Yucatán after the decline of <b>Chichen Itza</b>, which archaeology shows had lost its regional dominance by about 1100.</blockquote><a href="/empire/league-of-mayapan">League of Mayapan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Temple of the Warriors at <b>Chichén Itzá</b> in Yucatán closely resembles Pyramid B, though on a larger scale, and the two cities share colonnaded halls, chacmools and warrior imagery.</blockquote><a href="/empire/toltec-state">Toltec state · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Maya cities, where in 378 its warriors installed a new dynasty at Tikal; after Teotihuacan’s fall, the cult of the Feathered Serpent flourished at Tula, the city of the Toltecs, and at <b>Chichén Itzá</b> in Yucatán, whose Sacred Cenote received offerings of jade, gold and human lives.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesoamerican-religion">Mesoamerican religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Caves, as entrances to the underworld, and cenotes, as sources of water and rain, were sacred: the Sacred Cenote at <b>Chichén Itzá</b> received offerings of jade, gold, copal incense and human lives.</blockquote><a href="/culture/maya-civilisation">Maya civilisation · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li></ul></section>
